+* `[EXC-3]` *(changed in 0.9.9)* The compiler MAY remove a check only when it proves no conflicting
+  access can occur, in one of two ways. Either every access to the object in the interval goes through
+  one handle local that is not reassigned, their intervals are ordered, and either the interval
+  contains no call, no virtual or interface dispatch and no call through a callable value, or escape
+  analysis proves that local is the only handle to the object. Or no access that could make the check
+  fail is held (`[EXC-19]`) anywhere in the program: for a read, no held write to the same field or to
+  a whole object of its class family (the classes that share one root base class); for a write, no
+  held access to either. The second way needs the whole program in view, so a program that calls
+  through `dyn` or an interface keeps these checks. Conflicting accesses through one local that the
+  compiler can see are rejected at compile time instead (`E3080`, shape X1).
 * `[EXC-3a]` Every removed check is recorded, with the condition that justified it, and reported by
   `ember inspect --safety --elided-only`.
 * `[EXC-4]` A `let` field is subject to the same rules: `let` fixes the binding, not the value
